  "action_play_random": "Play a random track",
  "article": {
    "address": {
      "crossfade": "When the track changes, the old one keeps playing until the new one has loaded. Then they crossfade over three seconds.",
      "gesture": "Browsers usually block audio until the person has interacted with the page, so a `/play`{secondary} link opened from somewhere else lands on the track and waits for one click.",
      "play": "Every track has its own URL, and `/play`{secondary} at the end of it means the track is playing. Pause is a link back to the track, and Next track is a link to the next one, which is picked as soon as the current track starts. So back and forward work, and a copied link opens the same track."
    },
    "data": {
      "graphql": "With Audius, the plan was to ask GraphQL for the next track. I’d go back to that if the catalog grows.",
      "route": "This page doesn’t use GraphQL like the rest of the site. The catalog is compiled into the page, and the audio comes from the storage bucket through the site’s `/assets/static`{secondary} route."
    },
    "music": {
      "generated": "Before it played recorded tracks, the page generated its own music: a seeded synth playing chords and a melody over sampled piano. It was a lot of code to keep one station running, and I never got the piano samples into the bucket anyway, so I cut it.",
      "hosting": "I host them because a page can only analyze audio from its own origin, or from a server that explicitly allows it. Streamed from anywhere else, the tracks would play but the analyzer would get nothing.",
      "others": "I looked at Audius and Jamendo first because their catalogs are much bigger. The machines I built this on couldn’t reach either service, so I went with a collection I could host myself.",
      "source": "The tracks come from [$t(collection)]({{url}}): {{tracks}} tracks Bilal Tahir generated with Suno and released into the public domain. I copied them into the site’s storage bucket and serve them from there."
    },
    "picture": {
      "analyser": "For each frame, the page reads a few values from the audio analyzer: overall energy, low, mid and high frequency energy, brightness, and whether a note just landed. It also passes the shader a {{bands}}-band spectrum as a texture.",
      "colours": "The colors come from the stylesheet, a background and up to six colors for each theme, so the picture follows the light and dark themes. A Bayer dither on top matches the home page’s background.",
      "dwell": "A scene normally stays up for 24 to 38 seconds. If the music moves into a new section, it can change sooner, and the cut waits up to two and a half seconds for a note so it lands with the music.",
      "random": "The next scene is random, and each track gets its own camera drift and a warmer or cooler lean. An earlier version picked scenes by genre. I dropped that because it was more code and the result didn’t look any better.",
      "scenes": "The picture is one fragment shader with {{scenes}} scenes (pools, clouds, rings, bars, a tunnel, a hive, a bloom and a few others). It blends between two of them at a time."
    },
    "what_this_is": {
      "browser": "The page listens to the track, pulls a few numbers out of the audio and feeds them into a shader that draws the picture. There’s no machine learning and no server doing the work.",
      "experiment": "I made this for fun, and all of it runs in the browser."
    }
  },
